Ladybird keeps at most 500 alarms per extension (Chromium’s
AlarmManager::kMaxAlarmsPerExtension), with names of up to 1024 bytes
(kMaximumNameLength) and a minimum delay of a second (kDevDelayMinimum,
for unpacked extensions). Its kMaxAlarmsError, kNoScheduledTime,
kBothRelativeAndAbsoluteTime, and kErrorMaximumNameLength texts keep
Chromium’s wording, since extensions compare them. And a periodic alarm
comes again in line with its first time, as Chromium’s AlarmManager::
OnAlarm() schedules it after each time it fires.

Alarms live in memory alone, as Gecko’s ext-alarms.js keeps them, where
Chromium and WebKit (in its WebExtensionAlarm) persist them. Ladybird
also schedules an alarm under a second for a second out, where Chromium
keeps the requested time and enforces the minimum with its timer.
